\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Taken from Fig. 1(a) in~\cite  {Li}.(a) The Hall-bar used for the measurements. It has a width of $w\approx 6.7\,\mu \text  {m}$ and contact spacing of length $l\approx 2\,\mu \text  {m}$.(b) Measured resistance $R$ as a function of temperature $T$ at zero magnetic field $B$.(c) Magnetoresistance measured at $T=2\,$K with $B$ applied from $\theta =0^\circ $ to $\theta =90^\circ $ (see inset).(d) Magnetoconductance as a function of the normal $B$ component, here the solid red line is a fit acquired using the HLN model.}}{4}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:li_1}{{3}{4}{Taken from Fig. 1(a) in~\cite {Li}.(a) The Hall-bar used for the measurements. It has a width of $w\approx 6.7\,\mu \text {m}$ and contact spacing of length $l\approx 2\,\mu \text {m}$.(b) Measured resistance $R$ as a function of temperature $T$ at zero magnetic field $B$.(c) Magnetoresistance measured at $T=2\,$K with $B$ applied from $\theta =0^\circ $ to $\theta =90^\circ $ (see inset).(d) Magnetoconductance as a function of the normal $B$ component, here the solid red line is a fit acquired using the HLN model}{figure.caption.3}{}}
